meta {
  name: 08. List Active Recipients
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/groups/{{settlementGroupId}}/members
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{token}}
}

docs {
  # List Active Recipients

  GET /api/v1/groups/:groupID/members reuses the existing group-members endpoint. This is a convenience request for settlement testing, not a new backend route. Set settlementGroupId manually.

  Response data is an array of active memberships with member_id, user_id, name, role (OWNER or MEMBER), joined_at. Use member_id for to_member_id; do not use user_id. The list may contain your own membership: exclude yourself in the frontend. Payment creation also requires the recipient account to be ACTIVE, even if an inactive account still has an ACTIVE membership in this existing list.

  Example (IDs and dates illustrative):

  ```json
  {
    "success": true,
    "data": [
      {"member_id":30,"user_id":3,"name":"Ali","role":"OWNER","joined_at":"2026-11-18T09:00:00Z"},
      {"member_id":31,"user_id":4,"name":"Reza","role":"MEMBER","joined_at":"2026-11-18T09:00:00Z"}
    ]
  }
  ```

  Copy the other user's member_id into settlementToMemberId yourself. No script changes the environment. The existing group endpoint permits group members or site admins; settlement endpoints additionally require active membership and provide no admin override.

  Errors follow the existing group endpoint: 400 BAD_REQUEST for an unparsable group ID, 401 token errors, 403 GROUP_FORBIDDEN, 404 GROUP_NOT_FOUND, 500 INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR. See Group / Get Group Members for full details.
}
